Using the Fourier transform property, \[X(\omega)=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}x(t)e^{-j\omega t}\,dt,\] \[\frac{dX(\omega)}{d\omega}
=-j\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}t\,x(t)e^{-j\omega t}\,dt.\] At \(\omega=0\), \[\left.\frac{dX(\omega)}{d\omega}\right|_{\omega=0}
=-j\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}t\,x(t)\,dt.\] Given \[x(t)=e^{-t^4}\cos t,\] both \(e^{-t^4}\) and \(\cos t\) are even functions. Hence \(x(t)\) is even, so \[t\,x(t)\] is an odd function. Therefore, \[\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}t\,x(t)\,dt=0.\] Thus, \[\left.\frac{dX(\omega)}{d\omega}\right|_{\omega=0}=0.\] \[\boxed{0}\]

